  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nAt 11 AM, the pressure gradient from San Diego to \nTonopah, NV was running 5.6 mb offshore. Winds had come down from \ntheir peak with only place with wind gusts over 30 mph was \ndirectly below the Cajon Pass. Earlier today gusts to 45 mph were \nobserved near the Cajon Pass. Weak offshore flow will continue \ninto Sunday, becoming weakly onshore by Monday. Given the \nunsettled weather quickly turning to offshore flow, marine layer \nlow clouds and fog will be absent from the coast this weekend. \nIt's possible low clouds could return to some locations Monday \nmorning, with higher confidence for low clouds returning by \nTuesday morning. \n\nHigh temperatures on Sunday are expected to be 3 to 5 degrees warmer \nthan today with another degree or two of warming expected on Monday. \nBy Monday, high temperatures in coastal areas will be 5 to 10 \ndegrees above average, with temperatures closer to average further \ninland. Temperatures will begin to trend below average by the end of \nthe week. \n\nGlobal ensembles are indicating the potential for unsettled weather \nfor the end of next week. There remains a lot of spread in solutions \nand uncertainty in the details. Rain chances are in the forecast as \nearly as Friday. In addition to precipitation, cooler, cloudier, and \nwindier conditions may develop. Please be prepared for more wet \nweather impacts, especially as you start to make those Halloween \nweekend plans.",
